+# Full study: the quadratic upper bound for 2-increasing triples
+
+## Frozen target and notation
+
+At pinned Formal Conjectures revision
+`2270d31e8dd611521f979de6d86da364930b7669`, the source declaration is
+
+```lean
+theorem maximalLength_le (n : ℕ) : F n ≤ n ^ 2
+```
+
+The local notation `F` denotes `maximalLength`, the supremum of lengths of
+lists of triples with every coordinate in `[1,n]` that are pairwise
+two-coordinate increasing.  The target changes no mathematics: it expands
+that notation and abstracts the declaration's explicit binder into
+`FrozenStatement := ∀ n : ℕ, maximalLength n ≤ n ^ 2`.
+
+## Anchored readable reconstruction
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:BEGIN -->
+### PU-00 — exact statement and definitions
+
+`Statement.lean` replays the source definitions `lt₂`, `IsIncreasing₂`, and
+`maximalLength` as `ltTwo`, `IsIncreasingTwo`, and `maximalLength`.  It then
+defines `FrozenStatement`, makes `Target` a transparent abbreviation, and
+gives identity transports in both directions.  Thus binder mode, domain,
+extremal set, inequality, and exponent are fixed before proof composition.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:BEGIN -->
+### PU-01 — collision obstruction
+
+Suppose two triples `a` and `b` agree in coordinates zero and one.  A witness
+to `ltTwo a b` would need two distinct coordinates on which strict increase
+holds.  Enumerating the three possible coordinates shows that at least one of
+those two witnesses must be coordinate zero or one, where strict increase
+contradicts equality.  This is `not_ltTwo_of_first_two_eq`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:BEGIN -->
+### PU-02 — finite grid and pigeonhole
+
+Assume `2 ≤ n`, every coordinate of every list member lies in `Set.Icc 1 n`,
+and the list has more than `n²` entries.  Map each list position `k` to
+`(s[k] 0, s[k] 1)`.  Its image lies in
+`Finset.Icc 1 n ×ˢ Finset.Icc 1 n`, a grid with exactly `n²` elements.  The
+finite pigeonhole principle therefore supplies distinct positions `i,j`
+whose first and second coordinates both agree.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:BEGIN -->
+### PU-03 — the case `2 ≤ n`
+
+If an admissible list were longer than `n²`, PU-02 would give a colliding pair
+of positions.  Exactly one of `i < j` and `j < i` holds.  Pairwise increase
+gives `ltTwo` from the earlier list member to the later one, while the two
+coordinate equalities (symmetrized in the second orientation) let PU-01
+refute it.  Hence every admissible list has length at most `n²`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:BEGIN -->
+### PU-04 — the case `n = 0`
+
+An entry in an admissible list would have coordinate zero in `Set.Icc 1 0`,
+which would assert `1 ≤ a 0 ≤ 0`.  That is impossible, so the list is empty
+and its length is `0 ≤ 0²`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:BEGIN -->
+### PU-05 — the case `n = 1`
+
+The range condition forces every coordinate of every entry to equal one.  If
+the list had two positions, pairwise increase would relate its first two
+members, but their first two coordinates agree; PU-01 yields a contradiction.
+Thus the list has fewer than two members and hence length at most `1 = 1²`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:BEGIN -->
+### PU-06 — bounded, nonempty supremum data
+
+PU-03, PU-04, and PU-05 combine into `admissible_length_le_sq`, so `n²` is a
+common upper bound for every length in the defining set.  The empty list is
+admissible for every `n` and has length zero, making that set nonempty.  These
+are exactly the boundedness and nonemptiness facts needed by `csSup_le`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:BEGIN -->
+### PU-07 — exact machine root
+
+For arbitrary `n`, unfold `maximalLength`.  The empty-list witness supplies
+nonemptiness, and an arbitrary defining-set member is bounded by
+`admissible_length_le_sq`; `csSup_le` proves
+`maximalLength n ≤ n²`.  Introducing the explicit `n` binder yields
+`theorem_complete : FrozenStatement`.  `Audit.lean` checks the direct type,
+both crosswalk directions, and the kernel axiom census.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:END -->
+
+## Supremum and trust details
+
+The supremum is over natural-number list lengths, not over an assumed
+extremal list.  Each arbitrary member is unpacked into its list, range proof,
+and pairwise-increase proof.  Consequently no existence of a maximizing list
+is postulated.
+
+Cold elaboration uses Lean 4.29.0 with `--trust=0`.  The exact root contains no
+placeholder, claim-specific axiom, unsafe declaration, or bodyless proof
+oracle.  Lean reports only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound` as
+transitive logical foundations; their exact disclosure remains subject to
+the canonical Master's independent foundation-profile review.
